1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:02,160
And now, to you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:09,240 --> 00:00:15,080
At a recent training event I was running, we had to break the participants up into patrols for the purposes of the training.

3
00:00:16,140 --> 00:00:20,820
And we had one troop had five or six people who were doing the training at the same time,

4
00:00:20,880 --> 00:00:24,080
and so we put them all on their own patrol, and then we had a couple other patrols.

5
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:29,760
So after we had done this, we went around the room and we asked each patrol what their name was,

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:31,800
and who their patrol leader would be.

7
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:39,420
I got to the one where all the people from the same troop were, and the youngest one, he must have been like 19 or 20 years old,

8
00:00:40,060 --> 00:00:44,680
the youngest one raised his hand kind of reluctantly when I asked who the patrol leader was.

9
00:00:44,720 --> 00:00:47,720
And I said, well, how did you guys decide on who the patrol leader would be?

10
00:00:47,820 --> 00:00:50,680
And he looked at me and he says, I was voluntold.

11
00:00:53,260 --> 00:00:55,120
Voluntold, that's a new word for me.

12
00:00:55,120 --> 00:01:01,660
But it was a very familiar feeling because I've been voluntold to do some things before in scouting.

13
00:01:01,780 --> 00:01:02,300
Do you get it?

14
00:01:02,900 --> 00:01:05,099
Volunteered and told kind of at the same time.

15
00:01:07,360 --> 00:01:10,620
Hey, that one is one for the Scoutmaster's lexicon, isn't it?

16
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:14,600
This is podcast number 15.

17
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:36,119
Hey!

18
00:01:36,240 --> 00:01:36,460
Hey!

19
00:01:36,460 --> 00:01:40,840
Hey!

20
00:01:40,899 --> 00:01:41,020
Hey!

21
00:01:41,020 --> 00:01:42,460
Yeah!

22
00:01:42,460 --> 00:01:44,539
My name is Paul O'Connor.

23
00:01:44,540 --> 00:01:48,700
Hello, welcome back to the Scoutmasters podcast.

24
00:01:48,960 --> 00:01:50,080
This is Clark Green.

25
00:01:51,080 --> 00:01:55,120
In just a moment, we'll listen to Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

26
00:01:55,380 --> 00:01:58,940
We're going to talk a little bit about kindness and compassion as a scout leader.

27
00:01:58,940 --> 00:02:03,440
Next, a camping story with a not-too-surprising ending.

28
00:02:04,020 --> 00:02:07,840
And then our fourth installment of Avoiding Eagle Scout Trauma.

29
00:02:08,200 --> 00:02:09,460
So let's get started, shall we?

30
00:02:11,460 --> 00:02:13,660
Scoutmastership in seven minutes.

31
00:02:14,540 --> 00:02:15,540
Or less.

32
00:02:23,880 --> 00:02:27,040
Over the last several podcasts, we've been talking about Eagle Scout drama.

33
00:02:27,040 --> 00:02:31,239
And our fourth installment of it is a little bit later on in this podcast.

34
00:02:31,320 --> 00:02:32,120
But it got me to thinking.

35
00:02:32,220 --> 00:02:39,560
I had an email from a set of parents whose son was subject to a lot of Eagle Scout drama.

36
00:02:40,480 --> 00:02:41,600
Not from the boy.

37
00:02:41,600 --> 00:02:46,519
The boy had done basically what he needed to do, but he was getting a lot of guff from his Scoutmaster.

38
00:02:47,180 --> 00:02:48,380
I just felt so bad.

39
00:02:50,040 --> 00:02:53,560
It happens pretty frequently, unfortunately.

40
00:02:53,560 --> 00:02:59,780
And I got to thinking about it, and I said, well, how do we keep boys responsible?

41
00:03:00,620 --> 00:03:03,480
How do we show them how to lead a life of integrity?

42
00:03:04,440 --> 00:03:12,260
How do we make them accountable without being shaming or letting anger take over?

43
00:03:12,780 --> 00:03:16,000
I mean, as a Scoutmaster, we're supposed to be an exemplary adult.

44
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:22,340
We're to bring all that experience and compassion that we've learned over the years to bear on the lives of our Scouts and to help them.

45
00:03:23,900 --> 00:03:31,020
We're supposed to rise above pettiness and bickering and anger by being mature and fair and even-handed.

46
00:03:31,020 --> 00:03:35,100
I got two answers for you.

47
00:03:35,380 --> 00:03:37,860
Be an adult and be kind.

48
00:03:38,880 --> 00:03:39,660
That's what does it.

49
00:03:40,700 --> 00:03:50,240
Scout-age boys are very susceptible to shame, especially from their peers, but also from their parents and their teachers and any other authority figure.

50
00:03:50,240 --> 00:03:59,720
I think if we really work at it, we will learn to redirect, correct, and counsel our Scouts without resorting to making them feel ashamed.

51
00:04:00,780 --> 00:04:02,620
Are there proper times to feel shame?

52
00:04:02,720 --> 00:04:03,020
Yes.

53
00:04:03,580 --> 00:04:03,940
Yes.

54
00:04:05,520 --> 00:04:12,100
But we shouldn't put ourselves into the role of the one who creates that shame, should we?

55
00:04:12,440 --> 00:04:12,740
No.

56
00:04:12,960 --> 00:04:19,940
We should be in the role of the one who understands when a boy is ashamed of himself and helps him look at it, like I said,

57
00:04:19,940 --> 00:04:22,040
with the benefit of our experience and our knowledge.

58
00:04:23,060 --> 00:04:24,600
You've been ashamed of yourself before.

59
00:04:24,700 --> 00:04:26,120
I've been ashamed of myself before.

60
00:04:26,240 --> 00:04:28,100
Well, we need to know how to get out of it.

61
00:04:28,860 --> 00:04:32,700
And we certainly don't need to be the source of making our Scouts feel ashamed.

62
00:04:33,340 --> 00:04:35,340
You know, compassion and kindness, they're not weaknesses.

63
00:04:35,540 --> 00:04:36,159
They're strengths.

64
00:04:36,500 --> 00:04:39,860
When we cultivate them, they overcome anger and shame.

65
00:04:39,860 --> 00:04:42,720
You know, if you're angry, it clouds your judgment.

66
00:04:43,260 --> 00:04:44,840
Compassion clarifies your judgment.

67
00:04:45,240 --> 00:04:48,080
Anger is a real negative, injurious, and scarring thing.

68
00:04:48,080 --> 00:04:52,640
When I get angry, I try to remember what an old preacher told me.

69
00:04:53,099 --> 00:04:54,219
Because you're going to get angry.

70
00:04:54,419 --> 00:04:56,020
I mean, you've got Scouts.

71
00:04:56,539 --> 00:04:57,359
They're of an age.

72
00:04:57,479 --> 00:04:59,919
They're going to do stupid things, and they're going to make you angry.

73
00:05:00,680 --> 00:05:02,080
Here's what the old preacher said.

74
00:05:02,419 --> 00:05:09,840
He said, you can't keep birds from flying over your head, but you can keep them from making a nest in your hair.

75
00:05:09,840 --> 00:05:15,520
So when you get angry and you're feeling emotional, stop.

76
00:05:16,260 --> 00:05:19,719
Understand that it's just a reaction.

77
00:05:20,960 --> 00:05:27,080
And then draw on your maturity as an adult to be kind and compassionate towards your Scouts.

78
00:05:27,080 --> 00:05:33,440
Angry, shaming Scoutmasters have angry, shaming, and scared Scouts.

79
00:05:35,780 --> 00:05:40,560
Compassionate, kind Scoutmasters, they have compassionate, kind Scouts.

80
00:05:40,659 --> 00:05:45,640
Now, which kind of Scout do you suppose will have a better record of behavior?

81
00:05:46,320 --> 00:05:50,780
Which kind of Scout will have a happier, more fruitful experience in Scouting?

82
00:05:51,219 --> 00:05:53,280
We're not standing in judgment over our Scouts.

83
00:05:53,280 --> 00:06:00,159
No, we're standing right next to them, helping them find the way through difficult times in their lives.

84
00:06:01,299 --> 00:06:09,900
And the exercise of a little bit of compassion and kindness will make your job as a Scoutmaster much more effective and much more gratifying.

85
00:06:23,280 --> 00:06:42,340
This has to be the truth, folks, because there is no way anyone could make this up.

86
00:06:42,340 --> 00:06:52,280
I want to pass a little hint along to you as a long-time camper, as a Scoutmaster.

87
00:06:53,479 --> 00:06:57,299
And that is, for the most part, stay away from canned meat.

88
00:06:58,880 --> 00:07:00,739
That's, you heard it, canned meat.

89
00:07:01,280 --> 00:07:01,539
Yeah.

90
00:07:01,539 --> 00:07:01,640
Yeah.

91
00:07:01,640 --> 00:07:13,039
All right, Spam.

92
00:07:13,659 --> 00:07:14,800
Everybody loves Spam.

93
00:07:15,800 --> 00:07:19,780
But, you know, it really should have a skull and crossbones on the label, right?

94
00:07:20,099 --> 00:07:24,440
I mean, it passes the digestive tract and goes right to the coronary arteries.

95
00:07:25,099 --> 00:07:25,300
Yeah.

96
00:07:25,300 --> 00:07:25,360
Yeah.

97
00:07:25,360 --> 00:07:25,460
Yeah.

98
00:07:25,460 --> 00:07:25,860
Yeah.

99
00:07:25,860 --> 00:07:26,360
Yeah.

100
00:07:26,360 --> 00:07:26,860
Yeah.

101
00:07:26,860 --> 00:07:27,360
Yeah.

102
00:07:27,360 --> 00:07:27,419
Yeah.

103
00:07:27,419 --> 00:07:27,479
Yeah.

104
00:07:27,479 --> 00:07:27,500
Yeah.

105
00:07:31,640 --> 00:07:44,460
The most interesting thing I remember about taking canned meat on a backpacking trip, my buddy and Andy and I were part of a group that was headed to Alaska.

106
00:07:44,599 --> 00:07:52,719
We were about 15 or 16, and we were doing a preliminary campout backpacking trip in the mountains of Shenandoah, Virginia.

107
00:07:54,820 --> 00:08:00,500
Andy and I were really pleased to be partners for the trip, and we had all this new gear and everything,

108
00:08:00,500 --> 00:08:01,840
and we were excited.

109
00:08:01,840 --> 00:08:09,640
And, you know, we got out there and we set up our campsite after we hiked in several miles and set up our tent and got our fire going.

110
00:08:10,240 --> 00:08:11,240
Oh, it was fantastic.

111
00:08:11,460 --> 00:08:12,680
We just sat around that fire.

112
00:08:12,780 --> 00:08:13,760
We were kings of the world.

113
00:08:20,320 --> 00:08:23,140
And we prepared a wonderful dinner and had that.

114
00:08:23,360 --> 00:08:28,200
Then we sat around reveling in how wonderfully skilled we were as outdoorsmen.

115
00:08:28,200 --> 00:08:32,740
And Andy remembered, hey, we have a can of those Vienna sausages.

116
00:08:33,799 --> 00:08:36,120
You remember Vienna sausages, don't you?

117
00:08:36,140 --> 00:08:37,480
I think you can still find them.

118
00:08:38,200 --> 00:08:42,539
I haven't actually bought a can of them in ages, but...

119
00:08:42,539 --> 00:08:48,260
You remember them.

120
00:08:48,319 --> 00:08:49,740
They have like a pop-top lid.

121
00:08:49,740 --> 00:08:56,500
You pop the top off and you see this kind of semi-transparent, congealed, gelatin-like grease on the top.

122
00:08:57,960 --> 00:08:58,440
Yeah.

123
00:08:58,880 --> 00:09:00,280
That was what we were talking about.

124
00:09:00,340 --> 00:09:03,700
So Andy took the can and put it on the grate over the fire to warm,

125
00:09:04,500 --> 00:09:11,340
because we could think of nothing better at the time than nice, warm Vienna sausages to top off just a wonderful day.

126
00:09:11,340 --> 00:09:24,040
And as we sit and reveled by the fire there in the next few minutes,

127
00:09:24,740 --> 00:09:30,920
the stillness of the wilderness was punctuated and broken by the sound of a pretty good-sized explosion.

128
00:09:30,920 --> 00:09:38,939
You guessed it, we didn't take the top off the Vienna sausages before we put them on the fire.

129
00:09:40,900 --> 00:09:42,920
But the miraculous part was...

130
00:09:44,360 --> 00:09:45,819
You know, the can?

131
00:09:46,319 --> 00:09:48,319
It was sitting right where we left it.

132
00:09:49,260 --> 00:09:53,540
We got our flashlights out, trying to figure out what that explosion was,

133
00:09:53,599 --> 00:09:55,000
and we looked at the can of sausages,

134
00:09:55,400 --> 00:09:59,900
and the inside of that can was as clean as if it had been licked by a dog.

135
00:09:59,900 --> 00:10:01,360
Look, I'm not kidding.

136
00:10:08,340 --> 00:10:09,760
We never found the top.

137
00:10:10,500 --> 00:10:12,079
Needless to say, we never found the sausages.

138
00:10:12,260 --> 00:10:16,560
I just, you know, kind of assume they're in low Earth orbit to this very day.

139
00:10:24,840 --> 00:10:28,020
So, yeah, beware of canned meats.

140
00:10:28,020 --> 00:10:32,060
And pop the top on it before you put it on the fire, okay?

141
00:10:32,060 --> 00:10:49,219
He's my favorite all-time Boy Scout.

142
00:10:52,280 --> 00:10:56,099
He's my favorite all-time Boy Scout.

143
00:10:56,100 --> 00:11:09,540
So this is the fourth in a series of discussions about avoiding Eagle Scout drama, and we've learned the following.

144
00:11:10,120 --> 00:11:20,600
We've learned that we need to avoid building up our own internal idea of what an ideal Eagle Scout is and trying to apply that to our Scouts.

145
00:11:21,160 --> 00:11:23,840
What we need to do is we need to teach them to do that.

146
00:11:23,840 --> 00:11:26,140
What's an ideal Eagle Scout in their opinion?

147
00:11:26,700 --> 00:11:29,120
What's a successful leader in their opinion?

148
00:11:29,760 --> 00:11:36,940
What is a successful activity level for them to be involved in scouting in their opinion?

149
00:11:37,860 --> 00:11:44,940
Now, if you work on this and you have these ongoing discussions and you feature this in the Scout Masters Conference,

150
00:11:45,020 --> 00:11:49,380
you're going to have six of these before you sit down to the Eagle Scout Conference.

151
00:11:49,380 --> 00:12:03,620
You will have a dialogue going back and forth, and you will be slowly but surely developing in a Scout an internalized standard that is going to be higher than the actual advancement standard.

152
00:12:03,620 --> 00:12:05,440
That's right.

153
00:12:05,440 --> 00:12:13,420
If you work at this and you're a responsible Scout Master, you're going to get them to build a standard for themselves.

154
00:12:13,660 --> 00:12:26,700
Again, I will repeat, an incredibly useful skill to have throughout life, to be able to look at a job, to have a standard of performance, and internalize that,

155
00:12:26,700 --> 00:12:38,320
and then to soar above that standard, to work hard to get to it, and then not to fall to pieces if you don't meet that internal standard,

156
00:12:38,820 --> 00:12:45,500
but to look at it, to observe, to see what's going on, and to make whatever adjustments are necessary

157
00:12:45,500 --> 00:12:49,680
so that your performance comes up to the standard that you have internalized.

158
00:12:49,680 --> 00:12:55,280
First, I want to talk in this fourth installment about the Eagle Project.

159
00:12:56,739 --> 00:12:59,099
And Eagle Projects are tough.

160
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:02,939
Not the doing part of the project.

161
00:13:03,359 --> 00:13:04,199
They can be challenging.

162
00:13:05,060 --> 00:13:11,760
But there has been this world of things that has grown up around Eagle Projects

163
00:13:11,760 --> 00:13:21,140
that serve, I think, in my experience, to confuse and to misdirect the efforts of an Eagle candidate.

164
00:13:21,860 --> 00:13:29,860
If you go onto the National Eagle Scout Association website and you download the Eagle Project workbook,

165
00:13:30,439 --> 00:13:34,880
you will find about two and a half pages of instructions about conducting the project,

166
00:13:35,200 --> 00:13:37,780
all said in less than about a thousand words.

167
00:13:39,120 --> 00:13:41,160
966 to be exact, because I count it.

168
00:13:42,540 --> 00:13:49,840
You Google the world's Eagle Scout project, and the last time I did that, I got 1.4 million references.

169
00:13:50,620 --> 00:13:57,980
You can avoid a lot of Eagle Scout drama surrounding the project by sticking with the 966 words in the workbook.

170
00:13:58,600 --> 00:14:00,180
I think that's adequate advice.

171
00:14:00,939 --> 00:14:04,380
If I were you, I would stop listening, I would just read the workbook, and I would move on.

172
00:14:04,620 --> 00:14:09,240
But what I'm going to do is I'm going to do the thing that I am kind of protesting against,

173
00:14:09,240 --> 00:14:15,700
because I'm going to add more to the vast commentaries on the Eagle Project,

174
00:14:16,500 --> 00:14:23,280
but I'm doing it out of the necessity in the hopes that it might help somebody avoid some drama.

175
00:14:23,280 --> 00:14:28,939
If your council advancement committee has amended the workbook, mine has.

176
00:14:29,540 --> 00:14:35,240
Just get a clean copy from the current workbook from the National Eagle Scout Association website.

177
00:14:36,300 --> 00:14:38,839
Make sure you have the correct iteration of the workbook.

178
00:14:39,060 --> 00:14:39,800
That's important.

179
00:14:39,800 --> 00:14:49,280
If you work, if along the way, someone like your district advancement chairman insists on something outside of the 966 words in the workbook,

180
00:14:49,900 --> 00:14:56,240
then just ask politely that they show you in black and white the language from the BSA, not the council advancement committee,

181
00:14:56,719 --> 00:15:00,240
from the BSA on which their instructions are based.

182
00:15:00,979 --> 00:15:03,579
If it's not in the workbook, it's not a part of the requirement.

183
00:15:03,580 --> 00:15:07,500
And here's the things about an Eagle Project that usually cause problems.

184
00:15:07,640 --> 00:15:08,820
One is originality.

185
00:15:09,060 --> 00:15:13,800
Now, I've had several Eagle Projects turned back by various district advancement chairmen

186
00:15:13,800 --> 00:15:17,880
because they say, well, it's not original, and somebody did a very similar project like that,

187
00:15:17,920 --> 00:15:19,280
and we'd like to mix it up a little bit.

188
00:15:19,840 --> 00:15:22,020
Let's go to the 966 words.

189
00:15:22,560 --> 00:15:24,680
This is what it reads right in the Eagle Scout workbook.

190
00:15:24,780 --> 00:15:27,360
Does the leadership service project for Eagle have to be original?

191
00:15:27,780 --> 00:15:30,200
Perhaps something you dream up that has never been done before?

192
00:15:30,200 --> 00:15:33,580
The answer, no, but it certainly could be.

193
00:15:34,320 --> 00:15:36,600
You may pick a project that has been done before,

194
00:15:36,840 --> 00:15:42,300
but you must accept responsibility for planning, directing, and following it through to its successful completion.

195
00:15:43,200 --> 00:15:46,920
I mean, there's a clear statement on the question of originality.

196
00:15:48,040 --> 00:15:48,520
No.

197
00:15:49,400 --> 00:15:55,900
Anyone who insists on originality is adding to the requirement, which we all know is a big no-no.

198
00:15:55,900 --> 00:16:03,819
The other problem is somebody will hand down an edict about a certain minimum number of hours being required for a project.

199
00:16:04,520 --> 00:16:06,860
It's a simple statement in the project workbook.

200
00:16:07,140 --> 00:16:10,240
It says no minimum number of hours is required.

201
00:16:11,020 --> 00:16:11,720
It says that.

202
00:16:12,380 --> 00:16:15,620
No target number of hours, no average number of hours.

203
00:16:15,620 --> 00:16:22,000
If stating these things were important to the Eagle Project, or if they were helpful,

204
00:16:22,660 --> 00:16:27,380
the folks at the BSA who have seen 2 million Eagle Scouts would have included a number.

205
00:16:28,120 --> 00:16:31,280
But there is no minimum number of hours required.

206
00:16:31,800 --> 00:16:33,460
How about the size of the Eagle Project?

207
00:16:33,580 --> 00:16:35,620
This is often a problem, too.

208
00:16:35,900 --> 00:16:38,060
Well, you really want to do a big Eagle Project.

209
00:16:38,300 --> 00:16:41,000
You know, it's going to be a big deal, so you want to make it big.

210
00:16:42,200 --> 00:16:43,200
Here's what it says.

211
00:16:43,200 --> 00:16:46,820
There are no specific requirements on size.

212
00:16:47,100 --> 00:16:47,820
Not one.

213
00:16:48,200 --> 00:16:48,440
Nada.

214
00:16:49,260 --> 00:16:51,780
This is what it exactly says in the workbook.

215
00:16:51,900 --> 00:16:52,220
Quote,

216
00:16:52,460 --> 00:16:54,240
How big a project is required?

217
00:16:54,620 --> 00:17:00,040
Well, there's no specific requirements as long as the project is helpful to a religious institution, school, or community.

218
00:17:00,420 --> 00:17:05,720
The amount of time spent by you in planning your project and the actual working time spent in carrying out the project

219
00:17:05,720 --> 00:17:11,020
should be as much as is necessary for you to demonstrate your leadership of others.

220
00:17:11,020 --> 00:17:17,460
It's up to the Eagle Candidate to design his project to adequately demonstrate his abilities.

221
00:17:18,839 --> 00:17:25,280
Another point that gets kind of confusing about Eagle Projects is the Scoutmaster's role in this whole process.

222
00:17:25,980 --> 00:17:28,800
What Scoutmasters do is they approve the project plan,

223
00:17:28,800 --> 00:17:34,960
and they may have a role in helping advise and coach the Scout through the planning process.

224
00:17:35,200 --> 00:17:40,120
But we need to remember an Eagle Candidate is conducting his project to demonstrate his leadership of others.

225
00:17:40,659 --> 00:17:43,820
Who will ultimately pass judgment on whether or not he's done that?

226
00:17:44,399 --> 00:17:45,419
Not the Scoutmaster.

227
00:17:45,420 --> 00:17:49,260
The Scoutmaster signs off to certify the project was, quote,

228
00:17:50,160 --> 00:17:53,360
planned, developed, and carried out by the candidate.

229
00:17:54,260 --> 00:17:54,740
Unquote.

230
00:17:55,140 --> 00:17:58,160
Not as to the quality of the project or the leadership.

231
00:17:58,760 --> 00:18:00,280
This isn't the Scoutmaster's concern.

232
00:18:00,900 --> 00:18:02,860
This is why we have an Eagle Board of Review.

233
00:18:03,520 --> 00:18:07,380
And they and they alone are going to evaluate the project,

234
00:18:07,740 --> 00:18:10,680
whether it was worthy of an Eagle, not the Scoutmaster.

235
00:18:11,780 --> 00:18:13,400
Eagle Projects may be simple.

236
00:18:13,400 --> 00:18:15,040
They may be complex.

237
00:18:15,800 --> 00:18:19,000
They might require thousands of dollars or not one red cent.

238
00:18:19,560 --> 00:18:22,620
They may take months or days to complete.

239
00:18:23,240 --> 00:18:28,340
They might require a whole bunch of hours on the part of a veritable army of helpers.

240
00:18:28,780 --> 00:18:32,400
They may have tremendous impact or simply might just make a difference.

241
00:18:32,980 --> 00:18:37,960
Every project in this broad spectrum is valuable, laudable, and important.

242
00:18:37,960 --> 00:18:44,120
There are few, if any, universal standards to the size, scope, and originality of the project.

243
00:18:44,660 --> 00:18:50,080
So every Scout, regardless of where he lives, his talents, abilities, or resources,

244
00:18:50,260 --> 00:18:52,360
has the opportunity to become an Eagle Scout.

245
00:18:53,020 --> 00:18:55,980
It's not that somebody at the BSA forgot to put the numbers in.

246
00:18:56,940 --> 00:18:58,920
They're not there for a good reason.

247
00:18:58,920 --> 00:19:03,480
I readily guide Scouts to explore what others have done for projects,

248
00:19:03,600 --> 00:19:07,200
and I encourage them to be imaginative and expansive when planning their projects.

249
00:19:07,340 --> 00:19:12,800
But I guard that my encouragements don't become coercive or don't become thought of as a requirement.

250
00:19:13,280 --> 00:19:16,940
I'm not placing myself on a pedestal telling you I'm the perfect Scoutmaster.

251
00:19:17,420 --> 00:19:19,940
I'm just trying to tell you what I have learned over time.

252
00:19:20,360 --> 00:19:22,960
Most of these things have come through making mistakes.

253
00:19:22,960 --> 00:19:27,020
Take this entire series into consideration.

254
00:19:27,600 --> 00:19:28,320
Do a little studying.

255
00:19:28,440 --> 00:19:29,260
Make sure I'm right.

256
00:19:29,780 --> 00:19:34,100
And hopefully it will help relieve Eagle Scout drama in your job as a Scoutmaster.

257
00:19:52,960 --> 00:20:02,540
Well, thank you for listening to another edition of the Scoutmaster podcast.

